Article 3 For the purposes of this Law, “works” means intellectual achievements with originality that can be expressed in a certain form in the fields of literature, art and science, including:
(1) written works;
(2) oral works;
(3) musical, dramatic, quyi, choreographic and acrobatic art works;
(4) works of fine art and architecture;
(5) photographic works;
(6) audiovisual works;
(7) graphic works such as engineering design drawings, product design drawings, maps and sketches, and model works;
(8) computer software;
(9) other intellectual achievements that are similar to works.

Article 5 This Law shall not apply to:
(1) laws, regulations, resolutions, decisions and orders of state organs, and other documents of a legislative, administrative or judicial nature, and their official translations;
(2) mere factual information;
(3) calendars, numerical tables, forms of common use and formulas.

Article 26 A user of another person's work shall conclude a license contract with the copyright owner, except where this Law provides that permission is not required.
A license contract shall include the following main items:
(1) the types of rights licensed;
(2) whether the licensed right is an exclusive or non-exclusive right of use;
(3) the territorial scope and term of the license;
(4) the standard and method of remuneration;
(5) liability for breach of contract;
(6) other items that the parties deem necessary to agree upon.

Article 27 The assignment of the rights provided for in subparagraphs (5) through (17) of the first paragraph of Article 10 of this Law shall be effected by a written contract.
A contract of assignment shall include the following main items:
(1) the title of the work;
(2) the types of rights assigned and the territorial scope;
(3) the price of assignment;
(4) the date and method of payment of the price;
(5) liability for breach of contract;
(6) other items that the parties deem necessary to agree upon.

Article 50 Technological measures may be circumvented in the following circumstances, but no technology, device or component for circumventing such measures may be provided to others, and other rights enjoyed by the right holder in accordance with law may not be infringed:
(1) for the purpose of classroom teaching or scientific research in schools, providing a small amount of published works for use by teaching or scientific research personnel, where such works are not available through normal channels;
(2) providing, without profit-making purposes, published works to persons with print disabilities in an accessible manner perceptible to them, where such works are not available through normal channels;
(3) state organs performing official duties in accordance with administrative, supervisory or judicial procedures;
(4) testing the security performance of computers, their systems or networks;
(5) conducting encryption research or reverse engineering research on computer software.
The provisions of the preceding paragraph shall apply to restrictions on rights related to copyright.

Article 51 Without the permission of the right holder, no one may perform the following acts:
(1) intentionally deleting or altering any rights management information on a work, typographical design, performance, sound recording or video recording, or broadcast or television program, except where such deletion or alteration is unavoidable due to technical reasons;
(2) knowing or having reason to know that the rights management information on a work, typographical design, performance, sound recording or video recording, or broadcast or television program has been deleted or altered without permission, still providing such work, etc. to the public.

Article 52 Anyone who commits any of the following infringing acts shall, depending on the circumstances, bear civil liability such as cessation of the infringement, elimination of the effects, making an apology, or compensating for losses:
(1) publishing a work without the permission of the copyright owner;
(2) publishing a work created in collaboration with another person as one's own work alone without the permission of the co-author;
(3) affixing one's name to another person's work for personal gain without participating in the creation;
(4) distorting or tampering with another person's work;
(5) plagiarizing another person's work;
(6) using a work by means of exhibition, production of an audiovisual work, or by means of adaptation, translation, annotation, etc., without the permission of the copyright owner, except where this Law provides otherwise;
(7) using another person's work without paying the remuneration that shall be paid;
(8) renting the original or a copy of a work or a sound recording or video recording without the permission of the copyright owner, performer, or producer of the sound recording or video recording of an audiovisual work, computer software, sound recording or video recording, except where this Law provides otherwise;
(9) using the typographical design of a book or periodical published by a publisher without the permission of the publisher;
(10) live broadcasting or publicly transmitting a live performance, or recording a performance, without the permission of the performer;
(11) other acts infringing copyright or rights related to copyright.

Article 53 Anyone who commits any of the following infringing acts shall, depending on the circumstances, bear the civil liability provided for in Article 52 of this Law; if the infringing act also harms the public interest, the competent copyright authority shall order the cessation of the infringement, issue a warning, confiscate the illegal gains, confiscate and destroy the infringing copies and the materials, tools, equipment, etc. mainly used for making the infringing copies, and if the illegal business turnover is 50,000 yuan or more, may also impose a fine of not less than one time but not more than five times the illegal business turnover; if there is no illegal business turnover, or if the illegal business turnover is difficult to calculate or is less than 50,000 yuan, may also impose a fine of not more than 250,000 yuan; if the act constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be investigated in accordance with law:
(1) reproducing, distributing, performing, screening, broadcasting, compiling, or disseminating to the public through an information network a work without the permission of the copyright owner, except where this Law provides otherwise;
(2) publishing a book for which another person has the exclusive right of publication;
(3) reproducing, distributing a sound recording or video recording containing a performance, or disseminating the performance to the public through an information network, without the permission of the performer, except where this Law provides otherwise;
(4) reproducing, distributing, or disseminating to the public through an information network a sound recording or video recording produced by a producer without the permission of the producer, except where this Law provides otherwise;
(5) broadcasting, reproducing, or disseminating to the public through an information network a broadcast or television program without permission, except where this Law provides otherwise;
(6) intentionally circumventing or destroying a technological measure, intentionally manufacturing, importing, or providing to others a device or component mainly used to circumvent or destroy a technological measure, or intentionally providing technical services to others for circumventing or destroying a technological measure, without the permission of the copyright owner or owner of rights related to copyright, except where laws or administrative regulations provide otherwise;
(7) intentionally deleting or altering any rights management information on a work, typographical design, performance, sound recording or video recording, or broadcast or television program, or knowing or having reason to know that such rights management information has been deleted or altered without permission and still providing the work, etc. to the public, without the permission of the copyright owner or owner of rights related to copyright, except where laws or administrative regulations provide otherwise;
(8) producing or selling a work that is counterfeit with the signature of another person.
